---
import Layout from "@/components/Layout.astro";
import { formatDate } from "@/lib/format";
import { meta } from "@/lib/load";
import { withBase } from "@/lib/paths";

const built = formatDate(meta.builtAt);
const files = [
  {
    href: withBase("/data/statements.csv"),
    name: "statements.csv",
    what: `One row per tracked agency (${meta.counts.agencies} rows): who it is, whether it has published, its dates, the Standard checklist and its current profile. Start here.`,
  },
  {
    href: withBase("/data/statements.json"),
    name: "statements.json",
    what: `The ${meta.counts.statements} published statements in full detail: the current profile (including the text of each commitment), and every version's classification, summary and changed answers.`,
  },
  {
    href: withBase("/data/timeline.json"),
    name: "timeline.json",
    what: `Every change we recorded (${meta.counts.revisions}, newest first), with its classification and summary.`,
  },
  {
    href: withBase("/data/adoption.json"),
    name: "adoption.json",
    what: "The monthly figures behind the charts on What the statements say, plus each agency's changes to those items and the version they happened in.",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Data downloads"
  description="Download the tracker's data (every agency, statement profile and change) as CSV and JSON."
>
  <div class="page data prose stack">
    <h1>Data downloads</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Everything on this site comes from these files. They are rebuilt every time the
      site is (this copy: {built}).
    </p>

    <h2 id="files">The files</h2>
    <dl class="files">
      {
        files.map((f) => (
          <div>
            <dt>
              <a class="mono" href={f.href} download>
                {f.name}
              </a>
            </dt>
            <dd>{f.what}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <h2 id="reading-csv">Reading the CSV</h2>
    <p>
      The columns come in four groups. <span class="mono">abbr</span> to{" "}
      <span class="mono">originality</span> describe the agency: name, portfolio,
      whether it must publish (<span class="mono">scope</span>), whether it has,
      statement URL, when we first saw it, how often it has changed, and its
      originality score. <span class="mono">stated_first_published</span> to{" "}
      <span class="mono">annual_review_overdue</span> are the statement&rsquo;s own
      dates and whether its annual review is overdue. The <span class="mono">standard_*</span>
      columns show which of the Standard&rsquo;s eight elements the statement covers.
      The rest are its current profile, using the answer lists in{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/ANUcybernetics/aps-ai-tracker/blob/main/src/aps_ai_tracker/profiles.py"
        >profiles.py</a
      >, with multiple values separated by semicolons.
    </p>
    <p>
      An agency with no statement has only the first group filled in, so a blank
      profile field means there was nothing to read, not that the answer was no.
    </p>

    <h2 id="caveats">Before you analyse</h2>
    <p>
      The profiles, classifications and summaries are a language model&rsquo;s
      reading, so check them against the statement text before you publish a
      finding. <a href={withBase("/reading")}>Reading the data</a> explains what
      each figure means. The record starts in November 2025, so a statement&rsquo;s
      first tracked version may not be its first published one.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each JSON file carries <span class="mono">generatedAt</span>,{" "}
      <span class="mono">headSha</span> and <span class="mono">schemaVersions</span>,
      so you can tell which version of the pipeline produced it. New CSV columns are
      added at the end.
    </p>

    <h2 id="not-here">Statement text</h2>
    <p>
      The text of every version is in the <a
        href="https://github.com/ANUcybernetics/aps-ai-tracker/tree/main/statements"
        >git repository</a
      >. The current statements are also published on the <a
        href="https://atproto.com">AT Protocol</a
      > network (which Bluesky runs on) by <a
        href="https://bsky.app/profile/apsaitracker.app">@apsaitracker.app</a
      >, with one record per statement and one per change, each with a permanent
      identifier. You can <a href="https://pdsls.dev/at://did:plc:yhnshyrc2iev6z65u3uraon4"
        >browse the records</a
      > or subscribe to the network&rsquo;s live feed.
    </p>

    <h2 id="terms">Licence</h2>
    <p>
      These files are <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"
        >CC BY 4.0</a
      >. See <a href={withBase("/about")}>About</a> for how to cite the tracker.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .files {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .files div {
    padding-block: var(--space-3);
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .files dt {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .files dd {
    margin: var(--space-1) 0 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    max-inline-size: var(--measure);
  }
</style>
